<article class="knowledge-card" id="master-26">
          <div class="card-head"><span class="section-no">26</span><h3>FRONTIERS &amp; DEEP QUESTIONS</h3><span class="chevron">⌄</span></div>
          <div class="card-body"><div class="topic"><div class="topic-name"><span class="topic-num">1</span>Origin of Consciousness</div><p>Subjective experience kaise arise hui? Open scientific + philosophical problem.</p></div><div class="topic"><div class="topic-name"><span class="topic-num">2</span>Origin of Life</div><p>Prebiotic chemistry se self-replicating systems tak transition kaise hua?</p></div><div class="topic"><div class="topic-name"><span class="topic-num">3</span>Unification in Physics</div><p>Quantum theory aur gravity ko ek deeper framework mein combine karna.</p></div><div class="topic"><div class="topic-name"><span class="topic-num">4</span>Dark Matter</div><p>Particle candidates, modified gravity aur other explanations ko test karna.</p></div><div class="topic"><div class="topic-name"><span class="topic-num">5</span>Dark Energy</div><p>Cosmological constant vs evolving explanations; observations se constrain karna.</p></div><div class="topic"><div class="topic-name"><span class="topic-num">6</span>Quantum Foundations</div><p>Measurement, locality, contextuality, interpretations aur information.</p></div><div class="topic"><div class="topic-name"><span class="topic-num">7</span>Emergence</div><p>Simple interacting parts se higher-level properties ka arise hona.</p></div><div class="topic"><div class="topic-name"><span class="topic-num">8</span>Complexity</div><p>Networks, feedback, nonlinear dynamics aur self-organization.</p></div><div class="topic"><div class="topic-name"><span class="topic-num">9</span>Simulation Hypothesis</div><p>Reality computationally simulated ho sakti hai kya? Philosophical/speculative idea.</p></div><div class="topic"><div class="topic-name"><span class="topic-num">10</span>Multiverse Hypotheses</div><p>Inflationary, quantum, string-landscape aur other proposals; evidence status varies.</p></div><div class="topic"><div class="topic-name"><span class="topic-num">11</span>Holography</div><p>Gravity, quantum information aur spacetime ke deep links.</p></div><div class="topic"><div class="topic-name"><span class="topic-num">12</span>AI Alignment</div><p>Advanced AI ko human goals/values ke saath reliably align karna.</p></div><div class="topic"><div class="topic-name"><span class="topic-num">13</span>Artificial General Intelligence</div><p>Broad flexible machine intelligence ka research concept; definition disputed.</p></div><div class="topic"><div class="topic-name"><span class="topic-num">14</span>SETI &amp; Extraterrestrial Life</div><p>Earth ke bahar life/intelligence ke evidence ki scientific search.</p></div><div class="topic"><div class="topic-name"><span class="topic-num">15</span>Future of Humanity</div><p>AI, biotech, climate, governance, space aur existential risks.</p></div><h4>SELF-TEST</h4><ul><li>Is section ka sabse basic question kya hai?</li><li>Iske 3 concepts ko ek doosre se connect karke samjhao.</li><li>Is field mein kya established hai aur kya abhi uncertain/open hai?</li></ul><p>Knowledge Multiverse — Master Roadmap                                                  Page 33</p><h4>THE 12 BIG QUESTIONS</h4><p>Reality: What exists, and what is fundamental?</p><p>Knowledge: How do we know what we know?</p><p>Mind: How does subjective experience arise?</p><p>Matter: What are the smallest physical constituents?</p><p>Space &amp; Time: What are space and time?</p><p>Life: How did life arise and diversify?</p><p>Universe: How did the cosmos begin and evolve?</p><p>Society: How should humans organize power, economy and justice?</p><p>Meaning: How do humans create or discover meaning?</p><p>Intelligence: What is intelligence, and can machines have it?</p><p>Value: What makes an action or life good?</p><p>Future: What kind of civilization should humanity build?</p><h4>ONE-YEAR STUDY PATH</h4><p>Months 1–2: Scientific method + mathematics + classical mechanics/electromagnetism.</p><p>Month 3: Chemistry + cell biology + evolution + genetics.</p><p>Month 4: Relativity + astronomy + cosmology.</p><p>Months 5–6: Quantum mechanics + quantum information.</p><p>Month 7: Neuroscience + psychology + consciousness.</p><p>Month 8: Logic + epistemology + philosophy of science.</p><p>Month 9: Western philosophy.</p><p>Month 10: Indian + Buddhist + Jain + Islamic/Sufi + Chinese/Japanese philosophy.</p><p>Month 11: Computer science + AI + economics + civics.</p><p>Month 12: Frontier topics: quantum gravity, origin of life, consciousness, AI alignment, complexity, multiverse.</p><p>Final principle: Curiosity rakho, lekin evidence ke bina certainty mat rakho. Science aur philosophy dono mein “I</p><p>don&#x27;t know yet” ek powerful answer hai.</p><p>Knowledge Multiverse — Master Roadmap                                                                              Page 34</p></div>
        </article>
<div id="advanced" class="divider"><span>02</span><h2>ADVANCED PHYSICS — “1%” ROADMAP</h2></div>
<article class="knowledge-card" id="advanced-0">
          <div class="card-head"><span class="section-no">START</span><h3>Introduction &amp; Study Framework</h3><span class="chevron">⌄</span></div>
          <div class="card-body"><p>THE KNOWLEDGE MULTIVERSE</p><p>ADVANCED PHYSICS + QUANTUM</p><p>PHYSICS — “1%” ROADMAP</p><p>A research-oriented map for a student who wants to go far beyond ordinary school-level lists.</p><p>The goal is not to memorize difficult words, but to build the mathematics, intuition and</p><p>problem-solving ability needed to understand frontier physics.</p><p>Important: “1%” here means unusually deep and uncommon for a school student—not a literal</p><p>measurement of intelligence or knowledge. Some topics below are university/research level and require</p><p>substantial mathematics.</p><p>How to use this roadmap</p><ul><li>Do not jump directly to String Theory or Quantum Gravity. Build the prerequisite chain.</li><li>For every topic, learn: definition → physical intuition → mathematics → derivation → experiment/evidence</li></ul><p>→ limitations/open questions.</p><ul><li>Keep a separate notebook for equations and another for conceptual questions.</li><li>Whenever possible, solve problems instead of only watching videos.</li></ul></div>
        </article>
<article class="knowledge-card" id="advanced-1">
          <div class="card-head"><span class="section-no">01</span><h3>Mathematical &amp; Conceptual Foundations</h3><span class="chevron">⌄</span></div>
          <div class="card-body"><p>Core mathematics</p><ul><li>Calculus: limits, derivatives, integrals, multivariable calculus</li><li>Linear algebra: vectors, matrices, eigenvalues/eigenvectors, Hilbert spaces</li><li>Complex numbers and complex analysis</li><li>Ordinary and partial differential equations</li><li>Fourier series and Fourier transforms</li><li>Probability, random variables and distributions</li><li>Vector calculus: grad, div, curl, Green/Gauss/Stokes theorems</li><li>Tensor notation and index notation</li><li>Symmetry, groups and basic representation theory</li><li>Variational calculus and the action principle</li></ul><p>Physics thinking</p><ul><li>Dimensional analysis and scaling</li><li>Order-of-magnitude estimation</li><li>Symmetry as a physical principle</li><li>Conservation laws and Noether&#x27;s theorem</li><li>Effective theories and approximations</li><li>Thought experiments vs experimentally testable claims</li></ul></div>
        </article>
<article class="knowledge-card" id="advanced-2">
          <div class="card-head"><span class="section-no">02</span><h3>Advanced Classical Mechanics</h3><span class="chevron">⌄</span></div>
          <div class="card-body"><p>Mechanics beyond Newton</p><ul><li>Lagrangian mechanics</li><li>Hamiltonian mechanics</li><li>Canonical momentum and phase space</li><li>Poisson brackets</li><li>Canonical transformations</li><li>Hamilton–Jacobi theory</li><li>Action-angle variables</li><li>Central-force motion and scattering</li><li>Rigid-body dynamics and Euler equations</li><li>Gyroscopic motion and precession</li><li>Nonlinear oscillators</li><li>Coupled oscillators and normal modes</li><li>Resonance, damping and driven systems</li><li>Chaos and sensitivity to initial conditions</li><li>Lyapunov exponents</li><li>Logistic map and routes to chaos</li><li>Ergodicity and mixing</li><li>Symplectic geometry in mechanics</li><li>Classical field theory</li></ul></div>
        </article>
<article class="knowledge-card" id="advanced-3">
          <div class="card-head"><span class="section-no">03</span><h3>Electromagnetism &amp; Advanced Field Physics</h3><span class="chevron">⌄</span></div>
          <div class="card-body"><p>From Maxwell to modern field ideas</p><ul><li>Maxwell equations in differential and integral form</li><li>Gauge freedom and gauge transformations</li><li>Potentials, gauge fixing and Lorenz/Coulomb gauges</li><li>Electromagnetic waves and dispersion</li><li>Radiation from accelerated charges</li><li>Liénard–Wiechert potentials</li><li>Multipole expansion</li><li>Green&#x27;s functions for electromagnetic problems</li><li>Waveguides and cavity modes</li><li>Plasma basics and collective electromagnetic behavior</li><li>Relativistic electromagnetism</li><li>Covariant formulation of electromagnetism</li><li>Electromagnetic stress-energy tensor</li><li>Classical field action and Lagrangian density</li><li>Gauge symmetry as a structural principle</li></ul></div>
        </article>
<article class="knowledge-card" id="advanced-4">
          <div class="card-head"><span class="section-no">04</span><h3>Special Relativity &amp; Relativistic Physics</h3><span class="chevron">⌄</span></div>
          <div class="card-body"><p>Spacetime</p><ul><li>Minkowski spacetime</li><li>Four-vectors and invariant intervals</li><li>Lorentz transformations</li><li>Rapidity and hyperbolic geometry</li><li>Relativistic energy-momentum relation</li><li>Relativistic Doppler effect</li><li>Relativistic velocity addition</li><li>Relativistic collisions</li><li>Proper time and worldlines</li><li>Relativistic kinematics of decays</li><li>Causal structure and light cones</li><li>Relativity of simultaneity</li><li>Classical relativistic field theory</li></ul></div>
        </article>
<article class="knowledge-card" id="advanced-5">
          <div class="card-head"><span class="section-no">05</span><h3>General Relativity &amp; Gravitation</h3><span class="chevron">⌄</span></div>
          <div class="card-body"><p>Gravity as geometry</p><ul><li>Equivalence principle</li><li>Curved spacetime and geodesics</li><li>Metric tensor</li><li>Christoffel symbols</li><li>Covariant derivatives</li><li>Curvature tensors</li><li>Riemann, Ricci and Einstein tensors</li><li>Einstein field equations</li><li>Stress-energy tensor</li><li>Geodesic deviation and tidal forces</li><li>Schwarzschild spacetime</li><li>Gravitational time dilation</li><li>Gravitational redshift</li><li>Perihelion precession</li><li>Light bending and gravitational lensing</li><li>Black-hole horizons</li><li>Kerr black holes</li><li>Frame dragging</li><li>Penrose process</li><li>Ergospheres</li><li>Gravitational waves</li><li>Binary inspirals</li><li>Cosmic censorship hypothesis</li><li>Singularity theorems</li><li>Black-hole thermodynamics</li><li>ADM energy and spacetime decomposition</li><li>Numerical relativity</li></ul></div>
        </article>
<article class="knowledge-card" id="advanced-6">
          <div class="card-head"><span class="section-no">06</span><h3>Quantum Mechanics: Deep Foundations</h3><span class="chevron">⌄</span></div>
          <div class="card-body"><p>Core quantum structure</p><ul><li>Quantum states and Hilbert spaces</li><li>Operators, observables and commutators</li><li>Born rule and probability amplitudes</li><li>Schrödinger equation</li><li>Time evolution and unitary operators</li><li>Picture changes: Schrödinger, Heisenberg and interaction pictures</li><li>Angular momentum algebra</li><li>Spin-1/2 systems and Pauli matrices</li><li>Addition of angular momenta</li><li>Identical particles and exchange symmetry</li><li>Bosons and fermions</li><li>Density matrices</li><li>Pure vs mixed states</li><li>Partial trace and reduced states</li><li>Quantum measurement theory</li><li>Projective measurements</li><li>POVMs and generalized measurements</li><li>Quantum decoherence</li><li>Open quantum systems</li><li>Lindblad master equation</li><li>Path integrals</li><li>Stationary phase and classical limit</li><li>Semiclassical approximation</li><li>WKB approximation</li><li>Quantum scattering theory</li><li>Born approximation</li><li>Partial-wave analysis</li><li>Resonances and metastable states</li></ul></div>
        </article>
<article class="knowledge-card" id="advanced-7">
          <div class="card-head"><span class="section-no">07</span><h3>Quantum Phenomena That Most Students Skip</h3><span class="chevron">⌄</span></div>
          <div class="card-body"><p>Unusual and high-value topics</p><ul><li>Bell inequalities and experimental tests of local realism</li><li>Kochen–Specker theorem</li><li>Contextuality</li><li>Leggett inequalities and nonlocal correlations</li><li>Delayed-choice experiments</li><li>Quantum eraser</li><li>Interaction-free measurement</li><li>Quantum Zeno effect</li><li>Quantum anti-Zeno effect</li><li>Geometric/Berry phase</li><li>Aharonov–Bohm effect</li><li>Topological phases of quantum systems</li><li>Anomalous weak values</li><li>Quantum teleportation</li><li>Superdense coding</li><li>Entanglement entropy</li><li>Mutual information in quantum systems</li><li>Monogamy of entanglement</li><li>Entanglement swapping</li><li>Quantum steering</li><li>Quantum error correction as physics</li></ul></div>
        </article>
<article class="knowledge-card" id="advanced-8">
          <div class="card-head"><span class="section-no">08</span><h3>Quantum Information &amp; Quantum Computing</h3><span class="chevron">⌄</span></div>
          <div class="card-body"><p>Information is physical</p><ul><li>Qubits and Bloch sphere</li><li>Quantum gates and circuits</li><li>Deutsch–Jozsa algorithm</li><li>Grover search</li><li>Quantum Fourier transform</li><li>Shor factoring algorithm</li><li>Phase estimation</li><li>Variational quantum algorithms</li><li>Quantum simulation</li><li>Quantum complexity: BQP and related classes</li><li>Quantum channels</li><li>Completely positive maps</li><li>Choi–Jamiołkowski isomorphism</li><li>Quantum error-correcting codes</li><li>Stabilizer formalism</li><li>Surface codes</li><li>Fault-tolerant quantum computation</li><li>Decoherence and error budgets</li><li>Quantum cryptography</li><li>Device-independent cryptography</li></ul></div>
        </article>
<article class="knowledge-card" id="advanced-9">
          <div class="card-head"><span class="section-no">09</span><h3>Quantum Field Theory (QFT)</h3><span class="chevron">⌄</span></div>
          <div class="card-body"><p>Particles as fields</p><ul><li>Why single-particle quantum mechanics is incomplete</li><li>Classical scalar, spinor and vector fields</li><li>Quantization of fields</li><li>Creation and annihilation operators</li><li>Fock space</li><li>Propagators</li><li>Vacuum fluctuations</li><li>Feynman diagrams as perturbative bookkeeping</li><li>Scattering amplitudes</li><li>Wick&#x27;s theorem</li><li>Canonical quantization</li><li>Path-integral quantization</li><li>Global vs local symmetries</li><li>Noether currents in field theory</li><li>Gauge theories</li><li>Spontaneous symmetry breaking</li><li>Goldstone theorem</li><li>Higgs mechanism</li><li>Renormalization</li><li>Running couplings</li><li>Renormalization group</li><li>Effective field theory</li><li>Anomalies</li><li>Instantons and topological sectors</li></ul></div>
        </article>
<article class="knowledge-card" id="advanced-10">
          <div class="card-head"><span class="section-no">10</span><h3>Standard Model &amp; Particle Physics</h3><span class="chevron">⌄</span></div>
          <div class="card-body"><p>Fundamental interactions</p><ul><li>Standard Model overview</li><li>Quarks and leptons</li><li>Gauge group SU(3)×SU(2)×U(1)</li><li>Quantum electrodynamics (QED)</li><li>Quantum chromodynamics (QCD)</li><li>Electroweak theory</li><li>Higgs field and Higgs boson</li><li>Color charge and confinement</li><li>Asymptotic freedom</li><li>Quark–gluon plasma</li><li>Neutrino oscillations</li><li>Neutrino mass and mixing</li><li>CKM matrix and CP violation</li><li>Flavor physics</li><li>Effective weak interactions</li><li>Deep inelastic scattering</li><li>Parton model</li><li>Collider physics</li><li>Precision tests of the Standard Model</li><li>Physics beyond the Standard Model</li></ul></div>
        </article>
<article class="knowledge-card" id="advanced-11">
          <div class="card-head"><span class="section-no">11</span><h3>Quantum Electrodynamics &amp; Advanced QFT</h3><span class="chevron">⌄</span></div>
          <div class="card-body"><p>Going deeper</p><ul><li>Feynman rules in QED</li><li>Tree-level scattering amplitudes</li><li>Loop corrections</li><li>Self-energy and vertex corrections</li><li>Vacuum polarization</li><li>Anomalous magnetic moment</li><li>Casimir effect</li><li>Running of the fine-structure constant</li><li>Dimensional regularization</li><li>Counterterms and renormalization schemes</li><li>Ward–Takahashi identities</li><li>Gauge fixing and Faddeev–Popov ghosts</li><li>BRST symmetry</li><li>Non-Abelian gauge theory</li><li>Instantons</li><li>Confinement and the mass gap problem</li></ul></div>
        </article>
<article class="knowledge-card" id="advanced-12">
          <div class="card-head"><span class="section-no">12</span><h3>Quantum Gravity &amp; Theoretical Frontiers</h3><span class="chevron">⌄</span></div>
          <div class="card-body"><p>Where current physics gets difficult</p><ul><li>Why quantum theory + general relativity conflict</li><li>Planck scale</li><li>Semiclassical gravity</li><li>Black-hole information problem</li><li>Hawking radiation</li><li>Page curve</li><li>Unitarity and information recovery</li><li>Firewall proposal</li><li>ER = EPR conjecture</li><li>Gauge/gravity duality</li><li>Holographic principle</li><li>AdS/CFT correspondence</li><li>Entanglement wedge reconstruction</li><li>Quantum extremal surfaces</li><li>Island formula</li><li>String theory basics</li><li>Extra dimensions</li><li>Compactification</li><li>Supersymmetry</li><li>Supergravity</li><li>M-theory</li><li>Branes</li><li>Loop quantum gravity</li><li>Spin networks</li><li>Spin foams</li><li>Causal dynamical triangulations</li><li>Asymptotic safety</li><li>Emergent spacetime</li><li>Quantum geometry</li></ul></div>
        </article>
<article class="knowledge-card" id="advanced-13">
          <div class="card-head"><span class="section-no">13</span><h3>Cosmology &amp; the Deep Universe</h3><span class="chevron">⌄</span></div>
          <div class="card-body"><p>From Big Bang to dark sectors</p><ul><li>Friedmann equations</li><li>FLRW spacetime</li><li>Cosmological redshift</li><li>Thermal history of the universe</li><li>Big Bang nucleosynthesis</li><li>Cosmic microwave background</li><li>Acoustic peaks</li><li>Cosmic inflation</li><li>Inflationary perturbations</li><li>Quantum fluctuations becoming cosmic structure</li><li>Dark matter candidates</li><li>Dark matter halos</li><li>Large-scale structure</li><li>Cosmic web</li><li>Dark energy</li><li>Cosmological constant problem</li><li>Accelerated expansion</li><li>Modified gravity</li><li>Primordial black holes</li><li>Reionization</li><li>21-cm cosmology</li><li>Gravitational-wave cosmology</li><li>Multiverse hypotheses and their testability</li><li>Anthropic reasoning in cosmology</li></ul></div>
        </article>
<article class="knowledge-card" id="advanced-14">
          <div class="card-head"><span class="section-no">14</span><h3>Condensed Matter &amp; Quantum Matter</h3><span class="chevron">⌄</span></div>
          <div class="card-body"><p>A major frontier often ignored by school students</p><ul><li>Crystal lattices and reciprocal space</li><li>Bloch theorem</li><li>Band structure</li><li>Fermi energy and Fermi surface</li><li>Semiconductors</li><li>Phonons</li><li>Second quantization in many-body physics</li><li>Superconductivity</li><li>BCS theory</li><li>Josephson effect</li><li>Superfluidity</li><li>Quantum Hall effect</li><li>Fractional quantum Hall effect</li><li>Topological insulators</li><li>Topological superconductors</li><li>Majorana zero modes</li><li>Dirac and Weyl semimetals</li><li>Spin liquids</li><li>Quantum magnetism</li><li>Many-body localization</li><li>Quantum phase transitions</li><li>Renormalization group in condensed matter</li><li>Emergent quasiparticles</li><li>Moire materials</li></ul></div>
        </article>
<article class="knowledge-card" id="advanced-15">
          <div class="card-head"><span class="section-no">15</span><h3>Plasma, Nuclear &amp; High-Energy Physics</h3><span class="chevron">⌄</span></div>
          <div class="card-body"><p>Extreme matter</p><ul><li>Plasma waves and instabilities</li><li>Magnetohydrodynamics</li><li>Fusion physics</li><li>Tokamak principles</li><li>Magnetic confinement</li><li>Inertial confinement</li><li>Nuclear binding energy</li><li>Shell model of the nucleus</li><li>Collective nuclear models</li><li>Nuclear decay and tunneling</li><li>Neutron stars</li><li>Equation of state of dense nuclear matter</li><li>Quark matter</li><li>Heavy-ion collisions</li><li>Quark–gluon plasma</li><li>Ultra-high-energy cosmic rays</li><li>Particle showers in matter</li><li>Neutrino astronomy</li></ul></div>
        </article>
<article class="knowledge-card" id="advanced-16">
          <div class="card-head"><span class="section-no">16</span><h3>Experimental &amp; Computational Physics</h3><span class="chevron">⌄</span></div>
          <div class="card-body"><p>How frontier physics is actually tested</p><ul><li>Interferometry</li><li>Laser cooling and trapping</li><li>Atomic clocks</li><li>Precision spectroscopy</li><li>Superconducting detectors</li><li>Single-photon detection</li><li>Bell-test experimental design</li><li>Particle detectors</li><li>Calorimeters and trackers</li><li>Accelerator basics</li><li>Synchrotron radiation</li><li>Gravitational-wave detectors</li><li>Noise, uncertainty and systematics</li><li>Monte Carlo methods</li><li>Numerical differential equations</li><li>Finite-difference methods</li><li>Finite-element methods</li><li>Data fitting and Bayesian inference</li><li>Machine learning for physics</li><li>Scientific reproducibility</li></ul></div>
        </article>
<article class="knowledge-card" id="advanced-17">
          <div class="card-head"><span class="section-no">17</span><h3>Philosophical Foundations of Physics</h3><span class="chevron">⌄</span></div>
          <div class="card-body"><p>Questions behind the equations</p><ul><li>What is a physical law?</li><li>Determinism vs indeterminism</li><li>Realism vs instrumentalism</li><li>Measurement and observer dependence</li><li>Arrow of time</li><li>Why is entropy linked to time?</li><li>Emergence and reductionism</li><li>Identity of particles</li><li>Locality and nonlocal correlations</li><li>Counterfactual definiteness</li><li>Fine-tuning and naturalness</li><li>Underdetermination by data</li><li>Role of mathematics in physics</li><li>Simulation hypothesis: philosophy vs evidence</li></ul></div>
        </article>
<article class="knowledge-card" id="advanced-18">
          <div class="card-head"><span class="section-no">18</span><h3>1% Study Challenges</h3><span class="chevron">⌄</span></div>
          <div class="card-body"><p>Do these only after learning the prerequisites</p><ul><li>Derive the Euler–Lagrange equation from an action</li><li>Derive Hamilton&#x27;s equations and connect them to phase space</li><li>Derive time dilation from Lorentz transformations</li><li>Use four-vectors to solve a relativistic collision problem</li><li>Calculate a geodesic in a simple curved metric</li><li>Explain why gravity can be described as geometry</li><li>Derive the uncertainty relation from operator commutators</li><li>Work with spin-1/2 states using matrices</li><li>Calculate a simple two-qubit entangled state</li><li>Prove a Bell inequality in a simplified setup</li><li>Understand density matrices and partial trace</li><li>Derive the harmonic oscillator using ladder operators</li><li>Study a particle in a finite potential well and tunneling</li><li>Learn the path-integral idea for a free particle</li><li>Understand the Dirac equation conceptually and mathematically</li><li>Derive the Klein–Gordon equation from relativistic energy</li><li>Learn why renormalization does not simply mean &#x27;removing infinity&#x27;</li><li>Explain spontaneous symmetry breaking</li><li>Understand Hawking radiation at the conceptual level</li><li>Study the Page curve and why it matters</li><li>Compare string theory, loop quantum gravity and holography without treating any as proven</li></ul><h4>Recommended progression</h4><p>→ Stage A: Class 11–12 mechanics, waves, thermodynamics, electricity, magnetism + calculus basics</p><p>→ Stage B: Lagrangian/Hamiltonian mechanics + special relativity + advanced electromagnetism</p><p>→ Stage C: Linear algebra + complex analysis basics + full non-relativistic quantum mechanics</p><p>→ Stage D: General relativity + statistical mechanics + condensed matter foundations</p><p>→ Stage E: Quantum information + quantum field theory + Standard Model</p><p>→ Stage F: Cosmology + black-hole physics + QFT in curved spacetime</p><p>→ Stage G: Quantum gravity, holography, string theory, loop quantum gravity and frontier research</p><p>Golden rule: Advanced physics is not about knowing more scary terms. It is about being able to ask</p><p>precise questions, derive results, understand experiments, spot assumptions, and know what is still</p><p>unknown.</p></div>
        </article>
<div id="challenges" class="divider"><span>03</span><h2>LEARNING PRINCIPLE</h2></div>
<div class="introbox"><h2>Golden rule</h2><p>Advanced knowledge ka matlab scary terms collect karna nahi hai. Precise questions poochna, results derive karna, experiments samajhna, assumptions spot karna aur jo abhi unknown hai usse clearly identify karna hai.</p></div>
